@@ -69,6 +69,31 @@ The :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log` methods offers the same capabilities as pr
 
 To help with debugging, the log() method has a ``log_locals`` parameter. If you set this to ``True``, Rich will display a table of local variables where the method was called.
 
+Printing JSON
+-------------
+
+The :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print_json` method pretty prints a JSON document supplied as a string. The output is valid JSON, indented with four spaces by default (set ``indent`` to change this). Output is syntax highlighted using the ``json.*`` styles in the theme, and highlighting can be disabled by setting ``highlight=False``. Here's an example::
+
+    >>> console.print_json('[false, true, null, "foo"]')
+
+Invalid JSON raises the error from the standard ``json`` parser, and nothing is printed.
+
+The :class:`~rich.json.JSON` renderable does the work of parsing, indenting, and highlighting, so you can also pass it to :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print` or :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log`::
+
+    >>> from rich.json import JSON
+    >>> console.print(JSON('{"name": "Rich"}'))
+
+The renderable disables wrapping on its text, so default printing crops lines wider than the console to the console width. To keep long lines whole, enable soft wrapping with ``soft_wrap=True``.
+
+There is also a package-level :func:`rich.print_json` function which uses the global console::
+
+    >>> from rich import print_json
+    >>> print_json('{"name": "Rich"}')
+
+To pretty print a JSON file from the command line, run the json module as a program with the path to the file::
+
+    python -m rich.json path/to/file.json
+
